The retained timings are usable as a **synthetic component benchmark**, with the following corrections. No files were changed or GPU workloads rerun.

1. **P2 — Qualify the “ceiling” claim.** B and C are surrogate workloads whose costs are not proven bounds on the eventual trainer. At `e1458d2`, `configure_model_compile` still rejects Kaggriculture; the probe invokes compilation directly. Describe the numbers as **conditional estimates for the specified A/B/C/D schedule**. The script also retains no finite-loss/gradient checks or sampling-versus-replay equality check, so this is timing evidence, not numerical qualification. [Results](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/results.md:213)

2. **P2 — Remove unsupported synchronization and bottleneck attribution.** The timer explicitly calls `end.synchronize()` before stopping the host clock. Host/event agreement therefore cannot establish intrinsically “fully synchronous” model calls. A−D measures incremental sampling-path cost, not isolated head kernels. B’s dominance does not isolate heads backward or Muon. No Nsight timeline exists; the reported `nsys` absence lacks a retained command-output receipt. [Timing harness](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/bench_model_sps.py:109)

3. **P2 — Correct the prelaunch and stopping claims.** The prelaunch receipt shows **563 MiB on both GPUs**, zero utilization and no visible compute processes—not zero memory. Launch followed one second later despite the statement’s memory-in-use rule. Also, the driver continues after failures and allows three separate 25-minute timeouts; it does not enforce first-failure stopping or a 45-minute aggregate limit. Actual execution succeeded in **227 seconds**, so these discrepancies do not invalidate the timings. [Receipt](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/pod/receipts/idle_nvidia_smi_prelaunch.txt:11), [driver](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/pod/run_all.sh:2)

4. **P2 — Separate memory observations from causal explanations.** Allocated peak **40.275 GiB** satisfies the component’s allocation target; the [plan explicitly defines 85% using `max_memory_allocated`](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md:31). Reserved peak **84.994 GiB / 89.5%** is a separate observation. Attribution to fragmentation and B carryover remains a hypothesis without allocator evidence or a phase-order control. Complete-trainer memory fit remains unqualified. [Memory explanation](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/README.md:55)

5. **P3 — Correct variability reporting.** Sparse B’s p90 is **148.678 ms versus 147.472 ms median: +0.818%**, contradicting “within 0.5% everywhere.” Dense B includes a **545.106 ms** sample versus **326.579 ms** median; disclose that tail. The weighted sum of component p90s is not a measured whole-update p90. [Sparse samples](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/pod/results_sparse.json:180), [dense outlier](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/pod/results_dense.json:169)

6. **P3 — Tighten method descriptions.** C’s trunk chunks were counted; its two head chunks were **calculated from source**. Sparse C is below the **packed**, not padded, bound. B’s scalar ratio is per-player joint clipping because each row represents one seat; the README’s “not per_player” wording is incorrect. The engine’s `0.1 × model time` allowance is conservative: exact 10% SPS loss permits `model time / 9`. [Method and limits](/Users/poonszesen/kg-v3/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/model-sps-ceiling-2026-09-29/README.md:35)

